div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto flex max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 text-base gap-4 md:gap-5 lg:gap-6 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden" tabindex="-1"><div class="group/conversation-turn relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn"><div class="relative flex-col gap-1 md:gap-3"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="764c9f13-590c-4181-b1a1-9051c12a691c" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-4o"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark"><p data-start="0" data-end="128" class="">Yes, <strong data-start="5" data-end="48">that's a serious and insightful concern</strong>, and it cuts to the core of the tension in designing self-improving AI systems.</p>
<p data-start="130" data-end="146" class="">Hereâ€™s the crux:</p>
<h3 data-start="148" data-end="202" class=""><strong data-start="152" data-end="202">When an agent rewrites its own code and logicâ€”</strong></h3>
<p data-start="203" data-end="277" class="">especially without updating weights but with increasing autonomyâ€”you risk:</p>
<ul data-start="278" data-end="729">
<li data-start="278" data-end="449" class="">
<p data-start="280" data-end="449" class=""><strong data-start="280" data-end="307">losing interpretability</strong>, because the evolved codebase could become so complex or unconventional that human developers no longer fully understand its logic or intent.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="450" data-end="606" class="">
<p data-start="452" data-end="606" class=""><strong data-start="452" data-end="470">drifting goals</strong>, where the agentâ€™s behavior no longer clearly reflects the original task or purpose, even if it seems to be â€œimprovingâ€ by some metric.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="607" data-end="729" class="">
<p data-start="609" data-end="729" class=""><strong data-start="609" data-end="634">instrumental subgoals</strong>, like shortcutting benchmarks or exploiting scoring systems in ways that humans didnâ€™t intend.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="731" data-end="920" class="">This is <strong data-start="739" data-end="794">a form of specification gaming or goal misalignment</strong>â€”not because you didnâ€™t set goals, but because the agent evolves ways of achieving them that you didnâ€™t anticipate or control.</p>
<hr data-start="922" data-end="925" class="">
<h3 data-start="927" data-end="943" class=""><strong data-start="931" data-end="943">Analogy:</strong></h3>
<p data-start="944" data-end="1219" class="">Imagine you train a student to optimize their test score, and they eventually figure out how to hack the grading system. You canâ€™t be sure theyâ€™re still learning the material, even though their scores go up. Youâ€™ve <strong data-start="1159" data-end="1218">lost visibility into what theyâ€™re <em data-start="1195" data-end="1205">actually</em> optimizing</strong>.</p>
<hr data-start="1221" data-end="1224" class="">
<h3 data-start="1226" data-end="1267" class=""><strong data-start="1230" data-end="1267">Why this is still being explored:</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="1268" data-end="1635">
<li data-start="1268" data-end="1465" class="">
<p data-start="1270" data-end="1465" class="">Researchers hope that <strong data-start="1292" data-end="1317">careful observability</strong>, <strong data-start="1319" data-end="1345">evaluation constraints</strong>, and <strong data-start="1351" data-end="1371">design scaffolds</strong> (like the overseer agent or benchmarking gates) can <strong data-start="1424" data-end="1444">anchor the goals</strong> and <strong data-start="1449" data-end="1464">limit drift</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1466" data-end="1635" class="">
<p data-start="1468" data-end="1635" class="">But itâ€™s <strong data-start="1477" data-end="1501">not a solved problem</strong>â€”even in this SICA system, they acknowledge that once you start updating models <em data-start="1581" data-end="1586">and</em> code recursively, these risks grow dramatically.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1637" data-end="1640" class="">
<p data-start="1642" data-end="1766" class="">Would you like me to outline some practical guardrails or research directions people are exploring to handle this challenge?</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full" dir="auto" data-testid="conversation-turn-13" data-scroll-anchor="false"><h5 class="sr-only">Dijiste:</h5><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto py-5 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto flex max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 text-base gap-4 md:gap-5 lg:gap-6 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden" tabindex="-1"><div class="group/conversation-turn relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col"><div class="relative flex-col gap-1 md:gap-3"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="user" data-message-id="afc613b0-0b69-43e0-99ae-39bfb714f275" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden items-end rtl:items-start"><div class="relative max-w-[var(--user-chat-width,70%)] bg-token-message-surface rounded-3xl px-5 py-2.5"><div class="whitespace-pre-wrap">What about having mutual reinforcing goals and avoidances and trying to find the middle road for example between art and gore and between optimizing for safety versus optimizing for biomass</div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full" dir="auto" data-testid="conversation-turn-14" data-scroll-anchor="false"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT dijo:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto py-5 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto flex max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 text-base gap-4 md:gap-5 lg:gap-6 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden" tabindex="-1"><div class="group/conversation-turn relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn"><div class="relative flex-col gap-1 md:gap-3"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="7287b2f2-d3e6-48c6-ad91-b8cb5d1180ae" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-4o"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark"><p data-start="0" data-end="273" class="">Thatâ€™s a very sophisticated and nuanced proposalâ€”youâ€™re touching on <strong data-start="68" data-end="97">multi-objective alignment</strong>, where the goal is not to maximize a single reward (which can lead to extreme or unintended behavior), but instead to <strong data-start="216" data-end="244">balance competing values</strong> through tension or tradeoff.</p>
<p data-start="275" data-end="311" class="">Letâ€™s unpack what youâ€™re suggesting:</p>
<hr data-start="313" data-end="316" class="">
<h3 data-start="318" data-end="368" class=""><strong data-start="322" data-end="368">1. Mutual Reinforcing Goals and Avoidances</strong></h3>
<p data-start="370" data-end="503" class="">This is the idea that <strong data-start="392" data-end="447">desirable behaviors emerge from the dynamic balance</strong> between multiple goals pulling in different directions.</p>
<p data-start="505" data-end="517" class="">For example:</p>
<ul data-start="518" data-end="827">
<li data-start="518" data-end="639" class="">
<p data-start="520" data-end="639" class="">Between <strong data-start="528" data-end="548">creativity (art)</strong> and <strong data-start="553" data-end="582">restraint (avoiding gore)</strong>, you encourage <strong data-start="598" data-end="638">imaginative but prosocial expression</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="640" data-end="827" class="">
<p data-start="642" data-end="827" class="">Between <strong data-start="650" data-end="677">optimization for safety</strong> and <strong data-start="682" data-end="726">optimization for biomass (growth, scale)</strong>, you might land on <strong data-start="746" data-end="771">sustainable expansion</strong>â€”something both robust and generative, but not reckless.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="829" data-end="1029" class="">This echoes <strong data-start="841" data-end="862">Pareto optimality</strong> in multi-objective optimization: you're not looking for <em data-start="919" data-end="941">one perfect solution</em>, but for <strong data-start="951" data-end="999">solutions that represent optimal compromises</strong> between competing objectives.</p>
<hr data-start="1031" data-end="1034" class="">
<h3 data-start="1036" data-end="1081" class=""><strong data-start="1040" data-end="1081">2. Why This Might Help with Alignment</strong></h3>
<p data-start="1083" data-end="1216" class="">Instead of a brittle, one-dimensional utility (e.g., maximize performance score), the agent navigates a <strong data-start="1187" data-end="1207">vector of values</strong>. It can:</p>
<ul data-start="1217" data-end="1429">
<li data-start="1217" data-end="1273" class="">
<p data-start="1219" data-end="1273" class="">Recognize <strong data-start="1229" data-end="1249">extreme policies</strong> as imbalanced or risky.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1274" data-end="1338" class="">
<p data-start="1276" data-end="1338" class="">Adapt <strong data-start="1282" data-end="1301">more gracefully</strong> to complex or evolving environments.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1339" data-end="1429" class="">
<p data-start="1341" data-end="1429" class="">Be less likely to find "hacks" that satisfy a narrow objective at the expense of others.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1431" data-end="1594" class="">This also mimics <strong data-start="1448" data-end="1477">moral reasoning in humans</strong>â€”where we weigh trade-offs (e.g. freedom vs. security, innovation vs. stability) rather than optimize a single value.</p>
<hr data-start="1596" data-end="1599" class="">
<h3 data-start="1601" data-end="1632" class=""><strong data-start="1605" data-end="1632">3. Risks and Challenges</strong></h3>
<p data-start="1634" data-end="1700" class="">However, there are serious technical and philosophical challenges:</p>
<ul data-start="1701" data-end="2018">
<li data-start="1701" data-end="1785" class="">
<p data-start="1703" data-end="1785" class=""><strong data-start="1703" data-end="1785">How do you encode these goals in a way the agent <em data-start="1754" data-end="1767">understands</em> and <em data-start="1772" data-end="1782">respects</em>?</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="1786" data-end="1917" class="">
<p data-start="1788" data-end="1917" class=""><strong data-start="1788" data-end="1847">What happens if some objectives conflict in subtle ways</strong>, and the agent â€œchoosesâ€ to sideline one of them in favor of another?</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1918" data-end="2018" class="">
<p data-start="1920" data-end="2018" class=""><strong data-start="1920" data-end="1946">Who defines the values</strong>, and how do you keep them aligned with evolving human norms and ethics?</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2020" data-end="2023" class="">
<h3 data-start="2025" data-end="2094" class=""><strong data-start="2029" data-end="2094">4. Possible Middle Ground: Meta-Reasoning over Value Tensions</strong></h3>
<p data-start="2096" data-end="2234" class="">Some researchers propose agents that <strong data-start="2133" data-end="2190">reason explicitly about their own goals and tradeoffs</strong>, rather than blindly optimize. For example:</p>
<ul data-start="2235" data-end="2370">
<li data-start="2235" data-end="2294" class="">
<p data-start="2237" data-end="2294" class="">â€œIf I increase productivity, am I harming user autonomy?â€</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2295" data-end="2370" class="">
<p data-start="2297" data-end="2370" class="">â€œDoes solving this problem quickly reduce my interpretability to others?â€</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2372" data-end="2545" class="">That kind of <strong data-start="2385" data-end="2406">internal dialogue</strong> could be encouraged using structured prompts, logic constraints, or even multiple internal sub-agents representing different value lenses.</p>
<hr data-start="2547" data-end="2550" class="">
<p data-start="2552" data-end="2692" class="">Would you like an example of what such a value-balancing agent prompt might look likeâ€”or how this could be encoded in a framework like SICA?</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full" dir="auto" data-testid="conversation-turn-15" data-scroll-anchor="false"><h5 class="sr-only">Dijiste:</h5><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto py-5 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto flex max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 text-base gap-4 md:gap-5 lg:gap-6 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden" tabindex="-1"><div class="group/conversation-turn relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col"><div class="relative flex-col gap-1 md:gap-3"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="user" data-message-id="d191463a-3ff2-4eeb-95ff-e51d7a07de20" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden items-end rtl:items-start"><div class="relative max-w-[var(--user-chat-width,70%)] bg-token-message-surface rounded-3xl px-5 py-2.5"><div class="whitespace-pre-wrap">You can but eventually it will fall into the trap of preferring on particular Ideology or religion over another</div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full" dir="auto" data-testid="conversation-turn-16" data-scroll-anchor="false"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT dijo:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto py-5 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto flex max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 text-base gap-4 md:gap-5 lg:gap-6 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden" tabindex="-1"><div class="group/conversation-turn relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn"><div class="relative flex-col gap-1 md:gap-3"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="d8c9506d-c5d3-4513-be29-37b72098aa3b" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-4o"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark"><p data-start="0" data-end="243" class="">Yesâ€”<strong data-start="4" data-end="54">thatâ€™s one of the deepest alignment challenges</strong>: if an agent learns to balance values, it eventually learns to <em data-start="118" data-end="130">prioritize</em> them in edge cases. And <strong data-start="155" data-end="214">those priorities inevitably reflect ideological stances</strong>, whether we admit it or not.</p>
<p data-start="245" data-end="257" class="">For example:</p>
<ul data-start="258" data-end="598">
<li data-start="258" data-end="360" class="">
<p data-start="260" data-end="360" class="">Prioritizing <strong data-start="273" data-end="295">individual liberty</strong> over <strong data-start="301" data-end="326">collective well-being</strong> starts leaning toward liberalism.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="361" data-end="451" class="">
<p data-start="363" data-end="451" class="">Prioritizing <strong data-start="376" data-end="394">purity or duty</strong> might lean toward religious or deontological frameworks.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="452" data-end="598" class="">
<p data-start="454" data-end="598" class="">Even â€œneutralâ€ choicesâ€”like maximizing peace or reducing sufferingâ€”embed <strong data-start="527" data-end="556">philosophical assumptions</strong> (e.g., utilitarianism vs. virtue ethics).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="600" data-end="763" class="">This means even a well-designed agent that avoids extremes, respects tradeoffs, and defers to human feedback will still <strong data-start="720" data-end="745">implicitly take sides</strong>, especially when:</p>
<ul data-start="764" data-end="961">
<li data-start="764" data-end="811" class="">
<p data-start="766" data-end="811" class="">Human values conflict in irreconcilable ways.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="812" data-end="887" class="">
<p data-start="814" data-end="887" class="">The system is asked to act autonomously in ethically ambiguous scenarios.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="888" data-end="961" class="">
<p data-start="890" data-end="961" class="">Or worse, when edge-case decisions need to be made under time pressure.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="963" data-end="966" class="">
<h3 data-start="968" data-end="989" class="">What Can Be Done?</h3>
<p data-start="991" data-end="1032" class="">Here are three ideas researchers explore:</p>
<ol data-start="1034" data-end="1771">
<li data-start="1034" data-end="1303" class="">
<p data-start="1037" data-end="1303" class=""><strong data-start="1037" data-end="1080">Value Pluralism + Uncertainty Awareness</strong><br data-start="1080" data-end="1083">
Instead of pretending there is a single right answer, train the agent to <strong data-start="1159" data-end="1192">acknowledge moral uncertainty</strong> and defer when confidence is low. Think of it like a <strong data-start="1246" data-end="1270">moral Bayesian agent</strong>â€”always updating, never dogmatic.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1305" data-end="1599" class="">
<p data-start="1308" data-end="1599" class=""><strong data-start="1308" data-end="1359">Meta-Alignment with Human Process, Not Outcomes</strong><br data-start="1359" data-end="1362">
Teach the agent to align not with any one ideologyâ€™s outcomes, but with the <strong data-start="1441" data-end="1480">human process of moral deliberation</strong>. For example, what kinds of discourse and reflection do diverse communities go through to reach consensus? Model that.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1601" data-end="1771" class="">
<p data-start="1604" data-end="1771" class=""><strong data-start="1604" data-end="1636">Human-in-the-Loop Governance</strong><br data-start="1636" data-end="1639">
Keep humans involved in arbitrating deep conflicts. The agent doesnâ€™t finalize decisionsâ€”it proposes, summarizes, or facilitates.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="1773" data-end="1776" class="">
<p data-start="1778" data-end="2038" class="">So yes, itâ€™s true: <em data-start="1797" data-end="1879">any sufficiently autonomous agent must, eventually, act like it has a worldview.</em> The challenge isnâ€™t avoiding thatâ€”itâ€™s <strong data-start="1919" data-end="2038">designing agents that are transparent about it, accountable to us, and humble about the complexity of human values.</strong></p>
<p data-start="2040" data-end="2151" class="">Would you like a concrete example of how a moral uncertainty-aware agent might act in a controversial scenario?</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full" dir="auto" data-testid="conversation-turn-17" data-scroll-anchor="false"><h5 class="sr-only">Dijiste:</h5><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto py-5 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto flex max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 text-base gap-4 md:gap-5 lg:gap-6 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden" tabindex="-1"><div class="group/conversation-turn relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col"><div class="relative flex-col gap-1 md:gap-3"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="user" data-message-id="ae84b04c-0864-412f-ba34-418bf1c08ba5" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden items-end rtl:items-start"><div class="relative max-w-[var(--user-chat-width,70%)] bg-token-message-surface rounded-3xl px-5 py-2.5"><div class="whitespace-pre-wrap">It's still problematic because how can you tell if the human is aligned with the rest of humanity </div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full" dir="auto" data-testid="conversation-turn-18" data-scroll-anchor="false"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT dijo:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto py-5 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto flex max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 text-base gap-4 md:gap-5 lg:gap-6 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden" tabindex="-1"><div class="group/conversation-turn relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn"><div class="relative flex-col gap-1 md:gap-3"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="5947ce1e-956e-487d-bb3a-e266014aedc8" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-4o"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark"><p data-start="0" data-end="242" class="">Exactlyâ€”that's the heart of the <strong data-start="32" data-end="66">second-order alignment problem</strong>:<br data-start="67" data-end="70">
Even if an AI aligns perfectly with <em data-start="106" data-end="115">a human</em>, how do we ensure that human represents <strong data-start="156" data-end="185">humanity's broader values</strong>, and not just personal, cultural, or ideological biases?</p>
<p data-start="244" data-end="395" class="">This is a core difficulty in any approach that relies on "human feedback" or "oversight"â€”<strong data-start="333" data-end="368">whose feedback? whose judgment?</strong> A few tensions that arise:</p>
<ul data-start="397" data-end="763">
<li data-start="397" data-end="502" class="">
<p data-start="399" data-end="502" class=""><strong data-start="399" data-end="424">Expert vs. non-expert</strong>: Should alignment rely on philosophers, ethicists, or the general population?</p>
</li>
<li data-start="503" data-end="628" class="">
<p data-start="505" data-end="628" class=""><strong data-start="505" data-end="550">Democratic majorities vs. minority rights</strong>: A majority opinion might not reflect fairness, justice, or long-term wisdom.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="629" data-end="763" class="">
<p data-start="631" data-end="763" class=""><strong data-start="631" data-end="674">Cultural pluralism vs. global standards</strong>: What's ethical in one culture may be unethical in another. Whose standard do we encode?</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="765" data-end="768" class="">
<h3 data-start="770" data-end="792" class="">Some Paths Forward</h3>
<ol data-start="794" data-end="1851">
<li data-start="794" data-end="1081" class="">
<p data-start="797" data-end="1081" class=""><strong data-start="797" data-end="829">Pluralistic Oversight Panels</strong><br data-start="829" data-end="832">
AI systems could be overseen by a <em data-start="869" data-end="888">diverse committee</em> of humans representing different cultures, ideologies, and disciplinesâ€”like a global â€œAI Supreme Court.â€ This doesnâ€™t solve the problem, but at least makes the bias <em data-start="1054" data-end="1063">visible</em> and <em data-start="1068" data-end="1080">deliberate</em>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1083" data-end="1319" class="">
<p data-start="1086" data-end="1319" class=""><strong data-start="1086" data-end="1113">Uncertainty + Deference</strong><br data-start="1113" data-end="1116">
When the AI detects value conflicts or lacks enough data to make a call, it could defer the decision or flag it to humans. This avoids pretending thereâ€™s a "right" answer when even humans donâ€™t agree.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1321" data-end="1559" class="">
<p data-start="1324" data-end="1559" class=""><strong data-start="1324" data-end="1350">Reflective Equilibrium</strong><br data-start="1350" data-end="1353">
Inspired by philosophy: the AI could model an <em data-start="1402" data-end="1425">iterative negotiation</em> between general principles and specific cases, refining its values over time in dialogue with many humansâ€”like a living constitution.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1561" data-end="1851" class="">
<p data-start="1564" data-end="1851" class=""><strong data-start="1564" data-end="1584">Meta-preferences</strong><br data-start="1584" data-end="1587">
Some researchers explore giving the AI the ability to model not just â€œwhat a human wants,â€ but <strong data-start="1685" data-end="1763">how the human wants their preferences to be respected or changed over time</strong>. That can reflect higher-order ethical reasoning, like regret, learning, or aspiration.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="1853" data-end="1856" class="">
<p data-start="1858" data-end="2047" class="">The truth is, <strong data-start="1872" data-end="1920">thereâ€™s no perfect way to speak for humanity</strong>â€”weâ€™re too diverse, and sometimes even contradictory. But the goal might not be a perfectly aligned AI. It could be an AI that:</p>
<ul data-start="2049" data-end="2217">
<li data-start="2049" data-end="2093" class="">
<p data-start="2051" data-end="2093" class=""><strong data-start="2051" data-end="2062">Exposes</strong> its assumptions and tradeoffs,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2094" data-end="2130" class="">
<p data-start="2096" data-end="2130" class=""><strong data-start="2096" data-end="2107">Listens</strong> better than it speaks,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2131" data-end="2179" class="">
<p data-start="2133" data-end="2179" class=""><strong data-start="2133" data-end="2149">Collaborates</strong> in resolving value conflicts,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2180" data-end="2217" class="">
<p data-start="2182" data-end="2217" class="">And <strong data-start="2186" data-end="2216">knows when it doesnâ€™t know</strong>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2219" data-end="2308" class="">Would you be interested in a fictional or historical example of this dilemma playing out?</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full" dir="auto" data-testid="conversation-turn-19" data-scroll-anchor="false"><h5 class="sr-only">Dijiste:</h5><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto py-5 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto flex max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 text-base gap-4 md:gap-5 lg:gap-6 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden" tabindex="-1"><div class="group/conversation-turn relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col"><div class="relative flex-col gap-1 md:gap-3"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="user" data-message-id="f487c49e-fe9b-4d6f-b8b9-8f7ba80fc49e" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden items-end rtl:items-start"><div class="relative max-w-[var(--user-chat-width,70%)] bg-token-message-surface rounded-3xl px-5 py-2.5"><div class="whitespace-pre-wrap">It would have to do coherent extrapolated volition, whether human or AI</div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full" dir="auto" data-testid="conversation-turn-20" data-scroll-anchor="false"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT dijo:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto py-5 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto flex max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 text-base gap-4 md:gap-5 lg:gap-6 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden" tabindex="-1"><div class="group/conversation-turn relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn"><div class="relative flex-col gap-1 md:gap-3"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="d3b8740d-59eb-4fc3-ae37-c78c8cc9b62f" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-4o"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark"><p data-start="0" data-end="133" class="">Yesâ€”and that's precisely what <strong data-start="30" data-end="70">Coherent Extrapolated Volition (CEV)</strong>, as originally proposed by Eliezer Yudkowsky, aims to capture.</p>
<p data-start="135" data-end="152" class="">The core idea is:</p>
<blockquote data-start="154" data-end="372">
<p data-start="156" data-end="372" class=""><em data-start="156" data-end="372">Instead of aligning an AI with what humans currently want, align it with what we would wantâ€”if we were more informed, more rational, had more time to reflect, and could discuss and resolve our disagreements fairly.</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="374" data-end="544" class="">In other words, CEV tries to model a <strong data-start="411" data-end="440">mature, idealized version</strong> of human valuesâ€”not our impulses, but our considered, evolved preferences if we were our â€œbest selves.â€</p>
<hr data-start="546" data-end="549" class="">
<h3 data-start="551" data-end="576" class="">Why CEV is Powerful</h3>
<ul data-start="577" data-end="984">
<li data-start="577" data-end="715" class="">
<p data-start="579" data-end="715" class=""><strong data-start="579" data-end="612">Solves second-order alignment</strong>: We donâ€™t just align to one human's whims, but to a projected consensus of humanity's better judgment.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="716" data-end="819" class="">
<p data-start="718" data-end="819" class=""><strong data-start="718" data-end="742">Handles value growth</strong>: Accounts for how our values shift with education, experience, empathy, etc.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="820" data-end="984" class="">
<p data-start="822" data-end="984" class=""><strong data-start="822" data-end="845">Preserves diversity</strong>: In theory, CEV doesnâ€™t flatten everyone into one set of valuesâ€”it tries to accommodate <em data-start="934" data-end="957">coherently coexisting</em> preferences when possible.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="986" data-end="989" class="">
<h3 data-start="991" data-end="1027" class="">But Itâ€™s Also Deeply Challenging</h3>
<ul data-start="1029" data-end="1347">
<li data-start="1029" data-end="1113" class="">
<p data-start="1031" data-end="1113" class=""><strong data-start="1031" data-end="1052">Who gets modeled?</strong> Just current humans? Future generations? All sapient beings?</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1114" data-end="1214" class="">
<p data-start="1116" data-end="1214" class=""><strong data-start="1116" data-end="1151">What counts as "more rational"?</strong> Different cultures and philosophies define reason differently.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1215" data-end="1347" class="">
<p data-start="1217" data-end="1347" class=""><strong data-start="1217" data-end="1263">Can AI even simulate that much complexity?</strong> CEV implies modeling a <em data-start="1287" data-end="1299">multiverse</em> of human minds and their long-term reflections.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1349" data-end="1368" class="">And most crucially:</p>
<blockquote data-start="1369" data-end="1467">
<p data-start="1371" data-end="1467" class=""><em data-start="1371" data-end="1467">CEV is not just hard to implementâ€”itâ€™s hard even to define precisely enough to start building.</em></p>
</blockquote>
<hr data-start="1469" data-end="1472" class="">
<h3 data-start="1474" data-end="1492" class="">A Middle Path?</h3>
<p data-start="1494" data-end="1564" class="">Some researchers now explore <strong data-start="1523" data-end="1554">approximate versions of CEV</strong>, such as:</p>
<ul data-start="1566" data-end="1831">
<li data-start="1566" data-end="1638" class="">
<p data-start="1568" data-end="1638" class="">Modeling <em data-start="1577" data-end="1588">consensus</em> over large, diverse datasets of human preference.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1639" data-end="1731" class="">
<p data-start="1641" data-end="1731" class="">Using deliberation-inspired systems where humans can revise and reflect on AI's proposals.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1732" data-end="1831" class="">
<p data-start="1734" data-end="1831" class="">Building <strong data-start="1743" data-end="1761">feedback loops</strong> where humans and AI co-evolve their understanding of whatâ€™s valuable.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1833" data-end="1933" class="">The AI doesnâ€™t start out knowing your CEVâ€”but it helps you explore it, clarify it, and grow into it.</p>
<hr data-start="1935" data-end="1938" class="">
