It was a huge waste of time, and really felt like an affront to my own expertise, which is... why I was hired.

Ricky is a senior game designer who says they were often asked to use AI on their last project, initially with the goal of saving time on basic tasks. One example they shared was being encouraged to use Chat GPT to do things like â€œgenerate outlines of existing games and their systems so that we could use them as references and discussion points. The issue? [The outlines] were often wrong, and I'd spend more time fixing them than if I'd just done it the old-fashioned wayâ€.

Since the AI was generating responses for games Ricky was already familiar with, they could easily see that those responses were fundamentally incorrect. â€When I say they were wrongâ€, Ricky says, â€ I mean they were talking about systems that didn't exist, items that didn't exist, and outlines of enemies that had incorrect explanations of their behaviourâ€.

â€œThere were more superfluous issues as well -- the whole thing read like marketing speak. There were buzzwords everywhere, it wasn't concise, and if I were to try and fix these issues using different prompts, or continuing my conversation with [the AI], well, then I'm just spending time prompt-engineering instead of just writing the fucking thing myself. It was a huge waste of time, and really felt like an affront to my own expertise, which is... why I was hiredâ€.

Ricky says they were also asked to use Chat GPT as an â€œidea generatorâ€, to help with tasks like â€œcoming up with a bunch of interesting puzzlesâ€ or â€œcreating ideas for enemiesâ€, supposedly to both save time and provide inspirational starting points. 

â€œIt really weighed on the creativity of my role, and again, spat in the face of my expertiseâ€, they say. â€œIt wasn't just this though; the tool itself lacks the intent, context, and limitations of what we're doing. It doesn't have other aspects of the project, influences, references, or personal experiences in the back of its mind, because it doesn't have a mind. Whenever we design something for a game, it's drawn from somewhere, influenced by other things, and filtered through our own experiences as a human. These AI ideas lose ALL of that, turning it into an omega-corporate â€˜back of the boxâ€™ list of ideas and featuresâ€.

I raised the concern that we shouldn't be doing any of this without consent from those actors...

Sally is an animator and 3D artist whose most recent video game work was as part of an indie studio working on a VR game. They say that AI tools made deep inroads into life at the studio, beginning with the gameâ€™s creative directors and lead designers â€œplaying around with Midjourneyâ€ before quickly deciding that they wouldnâ€™t need to hire concept artists, then moving on to declaring that they wouldnâ€™t need to pay anyone at all to create the gameâ€™s 2D assets, because those could just be generated by Midjourney then edited in Photoshop. 

Sally began voicing their concerns directly with management when AI moved into the gameâ€™s voice work, as bosses began toying with new AI-generated lines trained on work previously performed by voice actors. â€œI raised the concern that we shouldn't be doing any of this without consent from those actorsâ€, Sally says, â€œand was met with a very aggressive shutdown response which was that â€˜it wasn't going to shipâ€™ and would only be used as an internal tool, so it shouldn't be an issue at allâ€.

â€œThis is pretty awful for the voice actors because thereâ€™s not really anything they can do if a studio decides to do something like this. [Studios] already have a bunch of their voice work, and often voice actors will just send the entire recording of their lines over at once. So a studio will have multiple takes of each line, and sometimes different variations of the same line, which is kind of a perfect example for something you could feed through AIâ€.

Sally was recently laid off from the studio, along with a few of their coworkers, and says that as far as theyâ€™re aware the studioâ€™s plan for its next game is to use AI-generated animations trained on motion capture to replace the role of a human animator. â€œI think the last thing I want to add is just how frustrating it is having AI-generated art in a game thatâ€™s about to ship, alongside all the hard work that we human artists put into it. All because one of the directors wanted to cut down on â€˜wasting timeâ€™ or â€˜hiring one other personâ€™â€. 

[AI] has been an overwhelmingly negative and demoralizing force in my own personal workplace, no question about it.

Audrey is a veteran concept artist who has worked both as a freelancer and in-house at studios in the video game industry. With qualifications in both art and game design, they say that over the last few years the emergence of AI tech in the art world â€œhas been an overwhelmingly negative and demoralizing force in my own personal workplace, no question about itâ€.

â€œMy own use of AI in my current job has been minimal and against my own wishes and ethicsâ€, they say. â€œOnce in a blue moon I have been given a task that necessitated using AI to make very VERY quick concepts. And when I say â€˜necessitatedâ€™, I would be told explicitly to use AI and make a ton of images to pick from, and I could then â€˜make them look good laterâ€™. Depressing doesnâ€™t begin to cover itâ€.

Audrey says that their work deadlines are â€œbeing completely fucked up as wellâ€, as they will usually need to completely redraw AI-generated images that colleagues in other departments have relied on as placeholder art, which then shifts their own workflow and priorities. 

Audrey says their teamâ€™s overall response to AI tech has been â€œmixedâ€. â€œOnly a few specific people higher up like to use AI and will use it regularly, while the majority of the team is neutral or completely against using itâ€, they say. â€œMy own personal opinion was quite vocal. I tried to be clear about my stance on using AI, and so did the rest of the art team. I gave multiple reasons for our company to stop or minimize using it; Iâ€™d ask about replacing images that were AI placeholders or if there was a way we could use art already made instead, all of which had mixed resultsâ€.

They never hired anyone for the artist position, and are using AI-generated art for their games.

Alfie is an experienced 2D artist in the video game industry. They recently interviewed at a startup games studio with millions in funding, and came away deeply affected by AI without even working there.

â€œThis corporate, tech-backed startup near me were looking to hire an artistâ€, they say. â€œI went through their entire recruitment process, which took around five weeks, including a trip in person to visit them for several hours. I even took part in a brainstorming session for about an hour, where I ended up coming up with a production plan with some basic schedules. After around three weeks of not hearing anything back from them, they finally got in touch to say they wouldnâ€™t be progressing with my applicationâ€.

Alfie wonders if the studio might have simply been using them for some free art and production consultation. â€œThey never hired anyone for the artist position, and are using AI-generated art for their gamesâ€, they say. â€œThere are zero artists on their teamâ€.

Alfie has since joined a new studio, which had been experimenting with AI-generated imagery a few years back but abandoned its use entirely after finding the results neither accurate nor specific enough for in-game purposes. This studio changed course entirely, and Alfieâ€™s hiring was part of a heavy investment in human art.

â€œI guess they realised that AI imagery can't solve all of your problems, and they realise just how precious real human artists areâ€, Alfie says. â€œA human artist will research the material properly, with all the nuances of human emotion and logic, and ultimately produce art assets that are historically accurate, game specific and have soulâ€.

...we have no other choice but to look for mundane work and give up our passion...

Douglas is a voice actor whose jobs include recording lines for indie video games, and says their experiences discussing AI with potential employers "feels like screaming into a void". "I've gotten through to small indie teams", they say, "and some publishers have been empathetic and understanding when I try steering them away from AI, since it hurts already-struggling independent artists and actors. But it's always the larger corporations that are eerily silent and flat out seem to ignore anyone who wants to talk about the subject".

As an example, Douglas says Audible has recently begun trialling a system where actors can submit their recordings to an AI program that will duplicate their voice, then sell it to book authors for their audiobooks. Those actors will then receive a small percentage of any income the AI 'voice' generates, but Douglas can easily see that becoming problematic for the market, with existing (and upcoming) voice actors able to be easily replaced by anyone looking for a "quick and easy" solution.

I asked Douglas how they and their peers are feeling about this, and other challenges being presented by AI in the voice acting space, and their response was blunt. "Myself and a lot of my fellow voice actors are in the same boat", they say. "None of us feel like submitting our voice to an AI is worth the benefit, because it will destroy a lot of the human emotion we put into our work to help bring a story to life".

"A lot of us worked tirelessly to make even a fraction of a living doing something we genuinely love, and we're afraid of that being taken away from us, so it's looking like we have no other choice but to look for mundane work and give up our passion".

These are just some peopleâ€™s personal experiences, a small snapshot of how AI is affecting the games industry. While surveys and numbers can provide some sense of scale, individual experiences can highlight the precise ways AI is encroaching into peopleâ€™s workplaces and lives, how they feel about it, and how theyâ€™re responding.

As grim as some of these responses feel, I hope thereâ€™s at least some small solace to be taken from the fact that so many artists remain firm in their resistance to AI technology that devalues their work, and are championing human creators in the face of it.</div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full" dir="auto" data-testid="conversation-turn-22" data-scroll-anchor="false"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT dijo:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto py-5 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto flex max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 text-base gap-4 md:gap-5 lg:gap-6 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden" tabindex="-1"><div class="group/conversation-turn relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn"><div class="relative flex-col gap-1 md:gap-3"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="f0e76a25-1ce9-46c3-977c-b2fa7d6ddc6c" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-4o-mini"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark"><p data-start="0" data-end="492" class="">The article paints a clear picture of the struggles faced by many in the creative and tech industries, particularly in the video game sector, as AI technologies increasingly encroach on traditional roles. The personal stories shared by developers, artists, animators, and voice actors illustrate the growing tension between the creative processes that define human expertise and the push toward more automated, AI-driven workflows. Here are some key themes that emerge from these experiences:</p>
<ol data-start="494" data-end="2589">
<li data-start="494" data-end="867" class="">
<p data-start="497" data-end="867" class=""><strong data-start="497" data-end="528">Erosion of Creative Control</strong>: Many workers express frustration at how AI tools, instead of supporting human creativity, are being used to bypass traditional processes. In particular, artists and designers report that AI-generated content is often poorly understood and poorly integrated into the development pipeline, leading to time-wasting and compromised outcomes.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="869" data-end="1251" class="">
<p data-start="872" data-end="1251" class=""><strong data-start="872" data-end="906">Devaluation of Human Expertise</strong>: Thereâ€™s a clear sense that AI is being introduced not to enhance human work but to replace it, or at least reduce its perceived value. Developers, artists, and voice actors all feel that their work is being undermined by AI tools that don't understand context, intent, or emotional nuanceâ€”things that are central to human-driven creative work.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1253" data-end="1669" class="">
<p data-start="1256" data-end="1669" class=""><strong data-start="1256" data-end="1297">Lack of Understanding from Management</strong>: One recurring issue is that executives and tech-driven leaders may view AI as a "quick fix" or a cost-saving measure, without fully appreciating its limitations or the detrimental effect it could have on quality, morale, and creativity. This lack of understanding often leads to top-down decisions that ignore the expertise and concerns of those actually doing the work.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1671" data-end="2090" class="">
<p data-start="1674" data-end="2090" class=""><strong data-start="1674" data-end="1702">The Ethical Implications</strong>: For those in industries like voice acting, AI poses a direct ethical challenge. The idea that an AI could replicate a personâ€™s voice or artistic style without consent raises issues of exploitation, ownership, and fairness. In many cases, workers feel forced into situations where they have no choice but to comply with AI-driven demands, even if it feels like a betrayal of their craft.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2092" data-end="2589" class="">
<p data-start="2095" data-end="2589" class=""><strong data-start="2095" data-end="2124">Resistance and Resilience</strong>: Despite the growing pressure to adopt AI, many workers are resisting, holding fast to the value of human creativity. Whether itâ€™s developers pushing back against AI-generated code or artists refusing to use AI tools that devalue their process, thereâ€™s a strong sense of defiance. Some are even leaving companies that insist on using AI excessively, while others are advocating for better integration of AI in ways that support, rather than replace, human workers.</p>
</li>
</ol>
