<hr data-start="188" data-end="191" class="">
<h3 data-start="193" data-end="275" class=""><strong data-start="197" data-end="275">Why It Feels Like AGI Would Need to Know "the Entire History of the World"</strong></h3>
<ol data-start="277" data-end="1366">
<li data-start="277" data-end="581" class="">
<p data-start="280" data-end="581" class=""><strong data-start="280" data-end="305">Context is Everything</strong><br data-start="305" data-end="308">
Human reasoning draws on vast, layered, often unconscious context: cultural norms, historical references, cause-effect relationships spanning centuries. A system trying to reason like a human must access or infer similar contextâ€”or it risks sounding naÃ¯ve or incoherent.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="583" data-end="827" class="">
<p data-start="586" data-end="827" class=""><strong data-start="586" data-end="614">Symbol Grounding Problem</strong><br data-start="614" data-end="617">
Concepts like "justice" or "progress" are not universal constantsâ€”theyâ€™re historically and culturally grounded. Without some historical narrative, an AGI risks treating complex ideas as disconnected symbols.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="829" data-end="1157" class="">
<p data-start="832" data-end="1157" class=""><strong data-start="832" data-end="882">Narrative Compression vs. Exhaustive Knowledge</strong><br data-start="882" data-end="885">
While humans donâ€™t remember every event, our minds compress knowledge into usable stories and heuristics. A working AGI likely needs not all <em data-start="1029" data-end="1036">facts</em>, but a rich model of how the world works, has worked, and could workâ€”which is functionally similar to "knowing history."</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1159" data-end="1366" class="">
<p data-start="1162" data-end="1366" class=""><strong data-start="1162" data-end="1209">Emergent Concepts Require Historical Layers</strong><br data-start="1209" data-end="1212">
AGI wonâ€™t be able to reason well about ethics, economics, or politics without some layered timeline of how these ideas have developed and been applied.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="1368" data-end="1371" class="">
<h3 data-start="1373" data-end="1429" class=""><strong data-start="1377" data-end="1429">How the Patent Tries to Address This (Partially)</strong></h3>
