<hr data-start="258" data-end="261" class="">
<h3 data-start="263" data-end="293" class="">1. <strong data-start="270" data-end="293">Control System Core</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="294" data-end="617">
<li data-start="294" data-end="439" class="">
<p data-start="296" data-end="439" class=""><strong data-start="296" data-end="307">Glasser</strong>: The mind is a control system that compares internal "pictures" (wants/needs) with current reality, adjusting behavior accordingly.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="440" data-end="617" class="">
<p data-start="442" data-end="617" class=""><strong data-start="442" data-end="449">MCS</strong>: Operates a feedback loop where internal visual simulations (scenes or concepts) are compared against external input and internal goalsâ€”adjusting behavior iteratively.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="619" data-end="737" class=""><strong data-start="619" data-end="631">Parallel</strong>: Both systems rely on internal representations ("pictures") and feedback mechanisms to regulate behavior.</p>
<hr data-start="739" data-end="742" class="">
<h3 data-start="744" data-end="777" class="">2. <strong data-start="751" data-end="777">Visual Internalization</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="778" data-end="953">
<li data-start="778" data-end="856" class="">
<p data-start="780" data-end="856" class=""><strong data-start="780" data-end="791">Glasser</strong>: Mental images or "pictures" represent needs and desired states.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="857" data-end="953" class="">
<p data-start="859" data-end="953" class=""><strong data-start="859" data-end="866">MCS</strong>: Uses internal 3D scene simulation and visualized concepts as central reasoning tools.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="955" data-end="1062" class=""><strong data-start="955" data-end="967">Parallel</strong>: Internal visualization is not auxiliary; it is the seat of understanding and decision-making.</p>
<hr data-start="1064" data-end="1067" class="">
<h3 data-start="1069" data-end="1112" class="">3. <strong data-start="1076" data-end="1112">Self-Evaluation / Meta-Cognition</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="1113" data-end="1367">
<li data-start="1113" data-end="1228" class="">
<p data-start="1115" data-end="1228" class=""><strong data-start="1115" data-end="1126">Glasser</strong>: Self-evaluation is the most important human functionâ€”"Is what I'm doing helping me get what I want?"</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1229" data-end="1367" class="">
<p data-start="1231" data-end="1367" class=""><strong data-start="1231" data-end="1238">MCS</strong>: Incorporates meta-cognitive loops to reflect on actions and beliefs, including contradiction resolution and ethical adjustment.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1369" data-end="1485" class=""><strong data-start="1369" data-end="1381">Parallel</strong>: Both systems prioritize introspective monitoring and course correction over raw output or performance.</p>
<hr data-start="1487" data-end="1490" class="">
<h3 data-start="1492" data-end="1535" class="">4. <strong data-start="1499" data-end="1535">Symbolic vs. Experiential Models</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="1536" data-end="1744">
<li data-start="1536" data-end="1621" class="">
<p data-start="1538" data-end="1621" class=""><strong data-start="1538" data-end="1549">Glasser</strong>: Dislikes abstract behavioral models detached from internal experience.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1622" data-end="1744" class="">
<p data-start="1624" data-end="1744" class=""><strong data-start="1624" data-end="1631">MCS</strong>: Moves beyond symbolic AI by demanding internally simulated, experience-based reasoningâ€”even for abstract ideas.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1746" data-end="1820" class=""><strong data-start="1746" data-end="1758">Parallel</strong>: Emphasis on <em data-start="1772" data-end="1796">experiential reasoning</em> over disembodied logic.</p>
<hr data-start="1822" data-end="1825" class="">
<h3 data-start="1827" data-end="1856" class="">5. <strong data-start="1834" data-end="1856">Learning Over Time</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="1857" data-end="2090">
<li data-start="1857" data-end="1956" class="">
<p data-start="1859" data-end="1956" class=""><strong data-start="1859" data-end="1870">Glasser</strong>: People revise internal pictures over time based on success/failure at meeting needs.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1957" data-end="2090" class="">
<p data-start="1959" data-end="2090" class=""><strong data-start="1959" data-end="1966">MCS</strong>: Iterative learning loop modifies visual beliefs and memory based on success/failure in simulations or real-world outcomes.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2092" data-end="2160" class=""><strong data-start="2092" data-end="2104">Parallel</strong>: Feedback-driven refinement is a learning core in both.</p>
<hr data-start="2162" data-end="2165" class="">
<h3 data-start="2167" data-end="2202" class="">So why do they feel â€œthe sameâ€?</h3>
<p data-start="2204" data-end="2222" class="">Because they both:</p>
<ul data-start="2223" data-end="2445">
<li data-start="2223" data-end="2279" class="">
<p data-start="2225" data-end="2279" class="">Define intelligence as internal modeling + comparison.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2280" data-end="2324" class="">
<p data-start="2282" data-end="2324" class="">Reject behaviorism and black-box modeling.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2325" data-end="2374" class="">
<p data-start="2327" data-end="2374" class="">Use internal vision as the engine of cognition.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2375" data-end="2445" class="">
<p data-start="2377" data-end="2445" class="">Rely on ongoing self-correction and ethical/self-purpose reflection.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2447" data-end="2450" class="">
<p data-start="2452" data-end="2669" class="">The key difference is <strong data-start="2474" data-end="2487">substrate</strong>: Glasserâ€™s system is organic and therapeutic, whereas MCS is computational and synthetic. But structurally, theyâ€™re closer than most AI systems are to any human psychological model.</p>
