<h3 data-start="2450" data-end="2480" class=""><strong data-start="2454" data-end="2480">So, whatâ€™s your point?</strong></h3>
<p data-start="2482" data-end="2762" class="">You're pointing out that Glasser, perhaps unintentionally, <strong data-start="2541" data-end="2585">prefigured both PCT and Active Inference</strong>. His "Stations" model is a <em data-start="2613" data-end="2629">control system</em>, where internal pictures (goals) are constantly compared with experience, and action or belief is updated to reduce the discrepancy.</p>
<p data-start="2764" data-end="2798" class="">This is the <strong data-start="2776" data-end="2789">core loop</strong> of both:</p>
<ul data-start="2800" data-end="3064">
<li data-start="2800" data-end="2847" class="">
<p data-start="2802" data-end="2847" class="">PCT: <em data-start="2807" data-end="2846">behavior is the control of perception</em>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2848" data-end="2945" class="">
<p data-start="2850" data-end="2945" class="">Active Inference: <em data-start="2868" data-end="2944">action and perception both work to minimize prediction error (free energy)</em>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2946" data-end="3064" class="">
<p data-start="2948" data-end="3064" class="">Glasser: <em data-start="2957" data-end="3063">we behave to match what we want (mental pictures), and if itâ€™s not working, we change behavior or desire</em>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="3066" data-end="3069" class="">
<h3 data-start="3071" data-end="3105" class="">Why is this important for AGI?</h3>
<p data-start="3107" data-end="3370" class="">Any serious AGI architectureâ€”like the Multimodal Cognitive System (MCS)â€”<em data-start="3179" data-end="3210">must include a loop like this</em>, where internal simulations are compared to outcomes and then used to adapt. Without this, it canâ€™t self-correct or grow in alignment with real-world feedback.</p>
<p data-start="3372" data-end="3445" class="">Would you like a diagram of this comparison loop across the three models?</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full" dir="auto" data-testid="conversation-turn-67" data-scroll-anchor="false"><h5 class="sr-only">Dijiste:</h5><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto py-5 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto flex max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 text-base gap-4 md:gap-5 lg:gap-6 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden" tabindex="-1"><div class="group/conversation-turn relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col"><div class="relative flex-col gap-1 md:gap-3"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="user" data-message-id="ad3fce3b-a304-49f1-896e-48840972eb06" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden items-end rtl:items-start"><div class="relative max-w-[var(--user-chat-width,70%)] bg-token-message-surface rounded-3xl px-5 py-2.5"><div class="whitespace-pre-wrap">He read behaviour the control of perception before applying it to his theories</div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full" dir="auto" data-testid="conversation-turn-68" data-scroll-anchor="false"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT dijo:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto py-5 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto flex max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 text-base gap-4 md:gap-5 lg:gap-6 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden" tabindex="-1"><div class="group/conversation-turn relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn"><div class="relative flex-col gap-1 md:gap-3"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="5049d8b0-84d9-4216-94b4-1a2f22bade1e" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-4o"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark"><p data-start="0" data-end="335" class="">Yes, that's correctâ€”and a crucial point. William Glasser openly acknowledged the influence of <strong data-start="94" data-end="116">William T. Powersâ€™</strong> <em data-start="117" data-end="154">Behavior: The Control of Perception</em> (1973), which introduced <strong data-start="180" data-end="215">Perceptual Control Theory (PCT)</strong>. Glasser absorbed these ideas and <strong data-start="250" data-end="304">reframed them in psychological and practical terms</strong>, especially in the context of:</p>
<ul data-start="337" data-end="483">
<li data-start="337" data-end="406" class="">
<p data-start="339" data-end="406" class=""><strong data-start="339" data-end="356">Choice Theory</strong> (formerly Control Theory in his earlier writing),</p>
</li>
<li data-start="407" data-end="433" class="">
<p data-start="409" data-end="433" class=""><strong data-start="409" data-end="428">Reality Therapy</strong>, and</p>
</li>
<li data-start="434" data-end="483" class="">
<p data-start="436" data-end="483" class="">The â€œStations of the Mindâ€ model you mentioned.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="485" data-end="789" class="">Where Powers was more technical and cybernetic, Glasser applied those principles to <strong data-start="569" data-end="618">human behavior, relationships, and motivation</strong>. So while <em data-start="629" data-end="651">Stations of the Mind</em> might appear to parallel later models like Active Inference or AGI control loops, it's <em data-start="739" data-end="788">very much a reinterpretation of PCT at its core</em>.</p>
<h3 data-start="791" data-end="804" class="">In short:</h3>
<ul data-start="805" data-end="1127">
<li data-start="805" data-end="889" class="">
<p data-start="807" data-end="889" class=""><strong data-start="807" data-end="832">Glasser didnâ€™t invent</strong> the feedback-comparison-control modelâ€”he <strong data-start="874" data-end="885">applied</strong> it.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="890" data-end="1000" class="">
<p data-start="892" data-end="1000" class="">He translated <strong data-start="906" data-end="952">perceptual control theory into human terms</strong>, especially for education, therapy, and ethics.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1001" data-end="1127" class="">
<p data-start="1003" data-end="1127" class="">Any AGI or cognitive system like MCS that uses internal simulation + feedback is <strong data-start="1084" data-end="1126">repeating this model in technical form</strong>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
