<li data-start="1105" data-end="1227" class="">
<p data-start="1107" data-end="1227" class=""><strong data-start="1107" data-end="1138">Subjectivity isnâ€™t required</strong>; only <strong data-start="1145" data-end="1164">a feedback loop</strong> and <strong data-start="1169" data-end="1226">a means of altering the controller across generations</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1228" data-end="1391" class="">
<p data-start="1230" data-end="1246" class="">If a system can:</p>
<ol data-start="1249" data-end="1391">
<li data-start="1249" data-end="1268" class="">
<p data-start="1252" data-end="1268" class="">Sense something,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1271" data-end="1315" class="">
<p data-start="1274" data-end="1315" class="">Act to maintain it within a viable range,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1318" data-end="1391" class="">
<p data-start="1321" data-end="1391" class="">Be altered based on performance (either through learning or heredity),</p>
</li>
</ol>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1393" data-end="1492" class="">Then it meets the basic criteria for <strong data-start="1430" data-end="1458">proto-conscious behavior</strong> and <strong data-start="1463" data-end="1491">evolutionary improvement</strong>.</p>
<p data-start="1494" data-end="1687" class="">This is close to <strong data-start="1511" data-end="1551">Karl Fristonâ€™s free energy principle</strong>, where <strong data-start="1559" data-end="1589">minimally conscious agents</strong> are those that resist entropy by inferring and acting on their world to maintain their integrity.</p>
