<hr data-start="871" data-end="874" class="">
<h3 data-start="876" data-end="944" class=""><strong data-start="880" data-end="927">2. Active Inference / Free Energy Principle</strong> â€“ <em data-start="930" data-end="944">Karl Friston</em></h3>
<p data-start="946" data-end="1100" class=""><strong data-start="946" data-end="960">Core Idea:</strong><br data-start="960" data-end="963">
Organisms act to minimize surprise (free energy) by continuously updating internal models of the world and acting to confirm predictions.</p>
<p data-start="1102" data-end="1131" class=""><strong data-start="1102" data-end="1131">Connection to Daisyworld:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="1132" data-end="1499">
<li data-start="1132" data-end="1388" class="">
<p data-start="1134" data-end="1265" class="">Daisies don't have internal models, but the <strong data-start="1178" data-end="1262">population-level dynamics behave as if the system is minimizing prediction error</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start="1268" data-end="1388">
<li data-start="1268" data-end="1331" class="">
<p data-start="1270" data-end="1331" class="">Temperature too cold? Black daisies thrive (increase heat).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1334" data-end="1388" class="">
<p data-start="1336" data-end="1388" class="">Too hot? White daisies dominate (cool the planet).</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="1389" data-end="1499" class="">
<p data-start="1391" data-end="1499" class="">This resembles <strong data-start="1406" data-end="1433">perception-action loops</strong> without cognitionâ€”like a <strong data-start="1459" data-end="1498">distributed active inference system</strong>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1501" data-end="1733" class=""><strong data-start="1501" data-end="1517">Implication:</strong><br data-start="1517" data-end="1520">
While not explicitly model-based, Daisyworld functions like an <strong data-start="1583" data-end="1614">embodied Bayesian regulator</strong>. It models how <strong data-start="1630" data-end="1708">perception and action can emerge from evolutionary and ecological dynamics</strong>, even in simple systems.</p>
<hr data-start="1735" data-end="1738" class="">
<h3 data-start="1740" data-end="1752" class="">Summary:</h3>
<div class="_tableContainer_16hzy_1"><div tabindex="-1" class="_tableWrapper_16hzy_14 group flex w-fit flex-col-reverse"><table data-start="1754" data-end="2016" class="w-fit min-w-(--thread-content-width)"><thead data-start="1754" data-end="1789"><tr data-start="1754" data-end="1789"><th data-start="1754" data-end="1763" data-col-size="sm">Theory</th><th data-start="1763" data-end="1789" data-col-size="md">Relation to Daisyworld</th></tr></thead><tbody data-start="1826" data-end="2016"><tr data-start="1826" data-end="1905"><td data-start="1826" data-end="1836" data-col-size="sm"><strong data-start="1828" data-end="1835">IIT</strong></td><td data-start="1836" data-end="1905" data-col-size="md">Low integration, but potential if internal state complexity added</td></tr><tr data-start="1906" data-end="2016"><td data-start="1906" data-end="1929" data-col-size="sm"><strong data-start="1908" data-end="1928">Active Inference</strong></td><td data-start="1929" data-end="2016" data-col-size="md">Behaves like a model-free adaptive agent minimizing free energy (temperature error)</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="sticky end-(--thread-content-margin) h-0 self-end select-none"><div class="absolute end-0 flex items-end" style="height: 32.7031px;"><span class="" data-state="closed"><button class="bg-token-bg-primary hover:bg-token-bg-tertiary text-token-text-secondary my-1 rounded-sm p-1 transition-opacity group-[:not(:hover):not(:focus-within)]:pointer-events-none group-[:not(:hover):not(:focus-within)]:opacity-0"><svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon-md-heavy"><path fill-rule="evenodd" clip-rule="evenodd" d="M7 5C7 3.34315 8.34315 2 10 2H19C20.6569 2 22 3.34315 22 5V14C22 15.6569 20.6569 17 19 17H17V19C17 20.6569 15.6569 22 14 22H5C3.34315 22 2 20.6569 2 19V10C2 8.34315 3.34315 7 5 7H7V5ZM9 7H14C15.6569 7 17 8.34315 17 10V15H19C19.5523 15 20 14.5523 20 14V5C20 4.44772 19.5523 4 19 4H10C9.44772 4 9 4.44772 9 